Your task:

1. From the semantic grouping tables (F3) and related F1–F2 evidence, infer the main recurring semantic dimensions or distinctions that appear across languages in the truth-related domain.

2. For each proposed dimension:
   - define it in 2–3 sentences using only patterns visible in the data
   - list representative terms from at least 2 languages, if available
   - note whether the distinction appears:
     - clearly lexicalized
     - morphologically derived
     - merged with another grouping
     - weakly attested or unclear

3. Produce a summary table:

| Dimension Label | Informal Definition (data-based) | Representative Languages & Terms | Distribution / Notes |
|-----------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------|
| ...             |                                   |                                  |                      |

4. At the end, explicitly mark:
   - which dimensions are strongly supported cross-linguistically
   - which are tentative or weakly attested
   - which seem to split, merge, or vary across specific languages

Important constraints:

- Do NOT reference AOML or any predefined axes or operators.
- Do NOT use example dimensions supplied from prior theory.
- Work only with what the F1–F3 tables and related context actually show.
- Use neutral, empirical language that other researchers can audit directly.
- Do not force every language into the same dimensional inventory.
